{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1775511034,"nonce":"kTkvPoEeglBlTGm1hjqsH1w3Zi0=","title":"Add file signal fallback to SessionRunner","message":"Add file signal fallback to SessionRunner\n\n## Context\nSignal protocol Phase 3: add file-based signal detection to SessionRunner as a fallback for agents that cannot use MCP tools (e.g. OpenHands, weak models). Agents write files to /session/signal/ directory. SessionRunner polls for these files each iteration.\n\nTested working with OpenHands on host and Docker (see docs/signal-protocol-design.md, file signal experiment 2026-04-06).\n\n## Tests (write first, must fail before implementation)\n- tests/test_session_runner.py::test_file_signal_done_detected\n  Assert: when /session/signal/done exists, SessionRunner detects DONE signal and deletes the file\n- tests/test_session_runner.py::test_file_signal_checkpoint_detected\n  Assert: when /session/signal/checkpoint exists with text content, SessionRunner detects CHECKPOINT signal with the content\n- tests/test_session_runner.py::test_file_signal_question_detected\n  Assert: when /session/signal/question.json exists with {\"question\": \"what?\"}, SessionRunner detects QUESTION signal and sets up Q\u0026A flow\n- tests/test_session_runner.py::test_file_signal_dir_created_on_init\n  Assert: SessionRunner creates /session/signal/ directory if it doesn't exist\n- tests/test_session_runner.py::test_file_signal_not_detected_when_absent\n  Assert: when signal dir is empty, _check_file_signals returns None\n\n## Files\n- core/session.py (modify — add _check_file_signals method, call it in event loop)\n- core/constants.py (modify — add SIGNAL_DIR_NAME)\n\n## Implementation\n1. In core/constants.py, add:\n   - `SIGNAL_DIR_NAME = \"signal\"` (subdirectory of /session/)\n2. In SessionRunner.__init__(), create the signal dir:\n   - `self._signal_dir = self._state.session_dir / SIGNAL_DIR_NAME`\n   - `self._signal_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)`\n3. Add SessionRunner._check_file_signals() method:\n   ```python\n   def _check_file_signals(self) -\u003e str | None:\n       done_file = self._signal_dir / \"done\"\n       if done_file.exists():\n           done_file.unlink()\n           return \"DONE\"\n       question_file = self._signal_dir / \"question.json\"\n       if question_file.exists():\n           data = json.loads(question_file.read_text())\n           question_file.unlink()\n           return (\"QUESTION\", data.get(\"question\", \"\"))\n       checkpoint_file = self._signal_dir / \"checkpoint\"\n       if checkpoint_file.exists():\n           desc = checkpoint_file.read_text().strip()\n           checkpoint_file.unlink()\n           return (\"CHECKPOINT\", desc)\n       return None\n   ```\n4. Call _check_file_signals() in the event dispatch loop, before processing stdout events. If a signal is returned, handle it the same way as the corresponding @@MARKER@@ text signal.\n\n## Patterns to follow\nSee existing marker handling in core/session.py _dispatch_event().\nSee docs/signal-protocol-design.md \"Layer 2: File signals\" section.\n\n## Notes\n- File signals are checked via stat() calls — cheap, no inotify needed.\n- Signal files are unlinked after detection to prevent re-triggering.\n- question.json uses JSON for structured data; done and checkpoint are plain text.\n\nREQ: REQ-004","files":null}]}